# Template only -- placeholders, no real values. The actual file lives at /etc/avenlyo/web.env on
# the Hetzner host, mode 0640, owned by a dedicated user/group, and is never committed.
#
# This is RUNTIME (server-only) configuration for the web container. It is loaded via
# `env_file: /etc/avenlyo/web.env` in deploy/compose.yaml. Build-time, browser-facing NEXT_PUBLIC_*
# configuration is a SEPARATE concern -- see deploy/env/build.env.example -- because Next.js compiles
# those values into the client bundle at `next build` time, not at container start. Setting a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable in this file would have no effect on the already-built image.
#
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY must never appear in this file. Web only ever uses the anon key plus the
# caller's own session; the service-role key is an API-only secret (see api.env.example).

# The deployed commit SHA. Same value as the api container's AVENLYO_RELEASE -- see
# docs/deployment/hetzner-staging.md for how Compose supplies this.
AVENLYO_RELEASE=

# Server-only address the web app's appointment, billing and integration server actions call.
#
# NOT http://api:4000 any more. deploy/compose.yaml puts web and api on separate networks so that
# Caddy is the only container able to peer with the API -- that separation is what makes the API's
# trusted-proxy boundary genuinely one hop rather than "any private container". Caddy carries an
# internal-only listener on :8080 for exactly this traffic: unpublished, reachable from the compose
# networks alone, never from the host or the internet, and involving no TLS or public DNS.
#
# ACTION ON NEXT DEPLOY: a host still carrying AVENLYO_API_URL=http://api:4000 must be updated to
# the value below, or the dashboard's appointment, billing and integration actions stop resolving.
AVENLYO_API_URL=http://caddy:8080

# Shared with the API's copy of the same name (api.env.example) -- must be byte-identical on both
# sides, or every billing mutation fails closed. At least 32 characters.
AVENLYO_INTERNAL_BILLING_SECRET=

# --- OpenAI, server-only ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required by the *web* container, not only by the API. This surprises people, and it cost a
# staging run: the first publish on the Hetzner host failed with "OpenAI embeddings are not
# configured. Set OPENAI_API_KEY to publish or test knowledge." while /etc/avenlyo/api.env had the
# key all along. The web container had never been given it, because this template did not mention
# it.
#
# The reason is that three knowledge features run inside the Next.js server process, not in the API:
#
#   * publishing a reviewed import, which embeds every included document
#     (apps/web/lib/knowledge/service.ts creates the embedding provider in-process)
#   * "Test your knowledge" search, which embeds the query the same way
#   * Agent Test, which calls the model directly (apps/web/lib/agent/service.ts)
#
# It is optional at boot on purpose (apps/web/lib/knowledge/config.ts): the app starts and every
# other page works without it, and the three features above report themselves unconfigured instead
# of crashing. That is why its absence surfaced as a publish-time error rather than a failed deploy.
#
# Server-only. It must never be exposed as NEXT_PUBLIC_ anything and must never go in
# deploy/env/build.env -- Next.js inlines build.env values into the browser bundle, which would
# publish the key to every visitor.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Both default in code (gpt-5.6 and text-embedding-3-small), so leave them unset unless you are
# deliberately changing models. If you do set OPENAI_EMBEDDING_MODEL, set the identical value in
# api.env: the API embeds live conversation and voice queries against documents the web container
# embedded, and two different models produce vectors that do not compare.
# OPENAI_AGENT_MODEL=gpt-5.6
# OPENAI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small

# Deliberately absent: OPENAI_PROJECT_ID, OPENAI_REALTIME_MODEL, and OPENAI_WEBHOOK_SECRET. Nothing
# in apps/web reads them -- they belong to the API's voice and webhook paths (api.env.example).
# Stripe is unset everywhere at this stage and belongs to the API in any case.
